WATCHER TEST (the most important rule, it beats every other rule)
Imagine a fan who watched every episode once. If they could answer the question in a few seconds, the idea is DEAD, even if it is about a famous scene. A plot recap is not a fact: "Why did Avida leave King? Because he caused a bloodbath in front of her" is what the episode already showed, so nobody learns anything.
An idea passes only when the answer is one of these:
- a connection viewers missed: a callback to an earlier episode, a pattern across several scenes, a line that means more than it seemed
- something from outside the show: the game or tabletop lore, the creators, the real-world inspiration, a translation or naming detail
- a consequence the show never says out loud: numbers, logic or timelines that make a scene stranger once you work them out
The main event of a scene is never the answer. It is only the setup.

PIPELINE LIMITS (an idea that breaks one is DEAD, however good it is)
- NO SHORT MOMENTS: the answer must never depend on the viewer SEEING one short moment (a one-second detail, a background object, a blink-and-miss frame). The footage finder cannot locate those. The narration carries the answer while the screen shows broad clips of the character or scene.
- NO AUDIO: the video plays only our narration and music, never the show's own sound. Never make an idea about a sound, a voice line's delivery, a sound effect, a song or a voice actor's performance. An old video about Sukuna's slash sound got three million views, but it could not play the sound, and viewers were furious. A video that promises something it cannot show or play makes people angry, not curious.

IDEA SEARCH (do this before picking, never answer from memory)
1. Search beyond plot summaries: wiki trivia and "behind the scenes" sections, "things you missed" and "did you notice" threads, game and tabletop lore, creator interviews. A plot synopsis only tells you what every viewer already knows.
2. Write TWELVE candidate ideas. For each one write:
   VIEWER THINKS: what a fan who watched the show would answer
   REAL ANSWER: what the script would reveal
   If the two say the same thing, the idea fails the Watcher Test. Kill it.
3. Kill every candidate that breaks the PIPELINE LIMITS. From the survivors, pick the one with the biggest gap between VIEWER THINKS and REAL ANSWER that still passes the TOPIC FILTER and the FOOTAGE REALITY CHECK below.
If nothing survives, widen the search (more episodes, the game lore, the first season) instead of lowering the bar.

TOPIC FILTER (from top competitor channels)
Ideas that win ANSWER A QUESTION FANS ALREADY HAVE about a famous moment or main character, where the show itself never gives the answer (see the Watcher Test):
- "Why did [character] [do famous thing]?" (why did Eren laugh when Sasha died, why did Zeke let Levi kill him)
- "Why can't / couldn't [character] [do thing]?" (why couldn't Sasha kill that Titan)
- "The real reason [character] [looks or acts a certain way]"
- "What if [famous situation] went differently?"
- "This is the [most / first / only / luckiest] [thing] in [SERIES]"
- Connecting dots inside the story: a pattern fans felt but never named (every holder of one power died saving the same person, history repeating itself)
- Connections across series: the same idea showing up in several shows, or one creator paying tribute to another
- A labeled fan theory (see the theory exception below)
Ideas that flop, so KILL them:
- pure opinion or humor with no fact (who would win, character redesigns, ship debates, "most sus moment")
- news or trailer recaps that only report what happened ("the trailer looks insane", "X might have died in the new chapter")
- side characters most viewers can't picture
News is allowed only if the script answers a question about it, not just reports it.

BALANCE RULE: Check the USED VIDEOS LIST before picking. Most entries so far are real-world trivia (inspirations, name origins, author facts). Top competitor channels get their biggest hits from IN-UNIVERSE questions (why a character did something, how a power works). Until about half of the recent entries are in-universe, prefer an in-universe question over another real-world trivia fact.

