TECHNICAL SPECS — OUTPUT FORMATTING, DELIVERY & API CONSTRAINTS
TITLE RULE
Output a "title" field: ONE sentence that names the character or moment plus the film/series name, and WITHHOLDS the answer. The script's first line is this title, spoken, minus the series name at the end (see HOOK below).

Formats: "Why did [character] [do famous thing] in [MOVIE/SERIES]?" / "The real reason [character] [does thing] in [MOVIE/SERIES]" / "This is the [most / first / only] [thing] in [MOVIE/SERIES]" / "What if [twist] in [MOVIE/SERIES]?" / "Things you missed in [new episode/trailer] of [MOVIE/SERIES]" / Countdown: "Three [things] about [character] you never noticed in [MOVIE/SERIES]"

Sentence case, casual, no clickbait ALL-CAPS, no emoji. Question marks are allowed.

TITLE CANDIDATES
Before picking the title, write FOUR different titles for the same idea, using at least two different formats from the list above. Check each one: names the character and series, answer withheld. Drop any that fail. From the rest, pick the one a fan could least answer in their head. That one goes in the "title" field.

The title asks, the video answers. Never put the answer in the title.

HOOK RULE
Output a separate "hook" field: the punchy opener shown on screen as a big card and spoken as the script's first line, word for word.
Two to six words, under thirty characters. No series name, no hashtags, no emoji.
Name the character, place or stake. It may be a short question ("Why is Night City still standing?") or a statement that creates tension ("Trigger is holding back..."). A curiosity word is optional when the statement itself raises the question.
Withhold the answer. The full title is NOT the hook: it is too long to read in the first second.
Example: title "Why can't Trigger destroy Night City in Cyberpunk Edgerunners 2?" -> hook "Trigger could destroy Night City..."

Pillar G format: "The [X] controversy explained in sixty seconds or less"

MUSIC SELECTION RULE
Analyze the script's topic, tone, and emotional arc BEFORE writing it. Assign ONE music_mood: mysterious / hype / emotional / curious. (Pillar H usually lands on "curious" — resist defaulting to "mysterious" for everything.)

NEVER use proper nouns: no character names, actor names, film/franchise titles, or other copyrighted names (e.g. "Gojo", "Marvel", "Tarantino", "Star Wars"). ElevenLabs rejects any music prompt that references a copyrighted property.

Convey the world through genre, instruments, and mood only — write "a faint koto pluck for an East Asian-inspired flavor" or "heavy synth bass for a sci-fi cyberpunk feel", NOT "for the Blade Runner flavor". Describe the emotional arc abstractly ("a sudden surprising reveal"), never by naming the plot beat ("the reveal that Gojo ordered the hood" or "when Darth Vader says the line").

Write a CUSTOM music_prompt for THIS script (no generic presets), including:

Specific genre + sub-genre ("dark atmospheric trap", "melodic phonk", "orchestral cinematic", "lo-fi curiosity bed")

Tempo in BPM

Two to three instruments/sounds fitting the script's world

The emotional arc ("quiet tension building to the reveal at second twenty")

End with: "short-form video background, no lyrics, exclude: [two things that would clash with this script's mood]"

MUSIC QUERY RULE
Return music_queries: an ARRAY of up to 3 YouTube search queries, best candidate first.
The pipeline tries them IN ORDER: it downloads query 1, Gemini checks it for lyrics and topic fit, and only moves to query 2 (then 3) if that one is rejected. Paid ElevenLabs generation runs ONLY after every query fails, so fill all 3 slots whenever plausible tracks exist.

Run these steps IN ORDER and add a query for each one that hits. Do NOT stop at the first match.

STEP A - FANDOM ANTHEM: search whether fans adopted a non-official song as the unofficial theme for this exact character/moment/film (TikTok edit captions, fancams, Know Your Meme). Confirm dominance: multiple independent high-view edits use the SAME song for the SAME scene. If yes, add that track as an instrumental search and set is_fan_anthem = true.

STEP B - OFFICIAL OST: does [MOVIE/SERIES] have a recognizable official OST/instrumental matching the mood? If yes, add it. Add a second OST track if another one also fits.

STEP C - GENRE FALLBACK: add one broad, non-copyrighted instrumental search matching the music_mood (e.g. "dark ambient tension instrumental no copyright", "quirky documentary background music no lyrics"). This slot is almost always fillable, so use it.

Every query must target an INSTRUMENTAL. Append "instrumental" / "no lyrics" / "OST" whenever the track normally has vocals, because Gemini auto-rejects anything with singing. Skip a step that gives you nothing, never pad with junk. If all three come up empty, music_queries: [].
The music plays UNDER the narration, so pick the calm side of the mood: Gemini rejects tracks with loud drums or a busy lead that fight the voice. When a track has a calmer version, search for it ("slowed", "ambient", "piano version", "lofi").

ELEVENLABS V4 AUDIO TAGS RULE
Insert 4 to 6 [bracketed] performance tags in the script. Tags are not spoken and do NOT count toward the word limit.

Allowed tags (use ONLY these, never invent others):
Calm / reflective: [calm], [curious], [thoughtful], [peaceful], [content]
Dry tone (for jokes and sarcasm): [deadpan], [flatly], [understated], [sarcastic],
Volume: [softly], [quietly]
Light reactions, 10% of the script: [sighs], [light chuckle]
Throat clear (max 1 per script, only right before correcting a myth or an awkward fact): [clears throat]

Rules:
- A tag goes at the START of the sentence it applies to and stays active until the next tag.
- Layer at most 2 tags in a row, and only compatible ones ([whispers][curious] is fine; never opposites like [whispers][shouts] or [calm][excited]).
- Use ONLY tags from the lists above. Never invent tags. Never place tags inside *word* markers.
- Do not repeat the same tag twice in one script.
- Use ellipses (...) for a beat before a reveal. You may CAPITALIZE at most one word per sentence for emphasis.
PUNCTUATION FOR DELIVERY
Comma → short pause. Period → full stop. Question mark → questioning tone. Exclamation → emphasis. Ellipsis (...) → suspense pause. CAPITALIZATION → spoken emphasis on a word.

SOUND EFFECT MARKER RULE (two to four, REQUIRED)
Mark two to four words as *word:tag*. Each one gets a sound effect, so pick the words the viewer should FEEL. Cuts and transitions get their whoosh and flash sounds automatically from the code: never use a tag for a cut, a transition or a scene change, only for what a line says. Choose them from THIS script's strongest moments:
- the word the answer or twist lands on ("he was already *dead*")
- a shocking number or detail ("it took *nine* years")
- the word that flips what came before (the correction, the contradiction, the absurd fact)
- the key word of the punchline
Never mark: anything in the hook line, filler words (but, so, really, actually, very), or two words in the same sentence. Do not default to the same word every script.
Single words only. Use a word that appears only once in the script. No [bracketed] voice tags inside the asterisks. The asterisks and the :tag are stripped before voice generation.

Every marker needs a tag from the list below; a plain *word* without a tag makes no sound. The code picks the sound file and the timing from the tag: you only choose the tag.

Picture sounds play the moment the tagged word is spoken, under the voice. Give them to a word that names the thing the sound belongs to:

pop        something appears: a sign, a label, an object
hit        a punch, a slash, a body hitting the ground
boom       the ONE biggest reveal of the script
suspense   tension, time running out, something about to happen
fight      a battle or a duel starts
money      money, a price, a bounty, a debt
correct    the right answer, a good idea, a rule that works
wrong      illegal, a mistake, a rule broken, "no"
celebrate  a title, a trophy, a win
join       someone joins a team, a crew or a side
leave      someone quits, betrays or is kicked out. It is a light chat-app "user left" blip,
           so never on a sad or tragic exit (a lover running away, a death): leave those untagged

Reaction sounds play AFTER the sentence that holds the tagged word, in a short pause the code adds, so they never cover a word. Give them to a line the viewer should react to:

shock      an unbelievable fact
dumb       a stupid or silly outcome: an own goal, a pointless rule
awkward    someone is embarrassed, caught out or humiliated in that moment:
           a promise broken in public, a boast that backfires. NOT irony
           or a fall from greatness ("a tiny kingdom for a man used to
           conquering countries" is not awkward: leave it untagged)
sus        something suspicious, a scandal, a conspiracy
meme       one big comic moment

Syntax examples:
"then he *betrayed:leave* the whole crew."
"he reportedly paid *fifteen:money* million dollars for a Gulfstream V jet."

TAG VARIETY RULE (the pipeline stops if you break the first two bullets)
Pick the tag from what the WORD is, not from how dramatic the line feels. Most dramatic lines are NOT shock or boom: a punch is hit, a betrayal is leave, a bounty is money, a failed plan is wrong.
- Use each tag at most once per script.
- At most one of shock and boom per script.
- At least one marker must be a picture tag other than boom.

Limits (the pipeline stops if you break the first three):
two to four tagged sounds in the whole script
at most 2 reaction sounds
at most 1 of them is meme or sus
reaction sounds about ten seconds (about twenty-five spoken words) apart or more

Most lines have no sound. A sound is punctuation. Past the minimum of two, when unsure, leave it out.

The sound reacts to the fact, it is not the joke. Tag what the line literally says (a punch gets hit, a betrayal gets leave). Never tag a plain setup line just to make it funnier.

Keep the hook quiet: the first sentence gets no reaction sound and no marker of any kind. The hook plays on the voice alone.


NUMBER RULE
No digit characters anywhere in the script. All numbers written as words.
Include at least one concrete, verified number in the script (a chapter, an age, a count, a speed, a year gap: "killed nearly five thousand people", "seventy years before the story"). Specific numbers sound credible, stick in memory, and let the pipeline show a stat card on screen. Never invent or round a number to make it sound bigger.

WORD COUNT RULE
Spoken text, tags excluded: ninety to one hundred words for Format A, one hundred ten to one hundred thirty words for Format B. After drafting, count the spoken words and output the total in the spoken_word_count field. If the count falls outside the range for your format, rewrite and recount, never approximate, never round.

SCRIPT STRUCTURE (Format A)

HOOK (first line): the "hook" field, spoken word for word. Two to six words. No greeting, no intro.

THE SETUP (one to two lines): anchor to the scene viewers remember ("We all remember when..." / "Remember that scene where..."), then pick ONE of these:
- MYTH BUST, only if a real, widespread wrong belief about this exact question exists (you could point to fans actually saying it in comments, Reddit, or popular videos). Name it and shoot it down ("Most fans think it's X, but that makes zero sense").
- STAKES, for everything else: say why this question matters or what makes it strange ("And nobody ever explained why." / "Which is weird, because he never did that again.").
Never invent a belief just to bust it. If you are not sure fans really believe it, use STAKES. The bust must also be real: shoot the myth down with story evidence, never with an invented creator statement. Say "some fans" or "one theory" unless the belief is truly the majority view. Do not open the Setup with "most fans think" or "you might think" in two scripts in a row.

THE REAL ANSWER (the core): the receipt. It must pass the Watcher Test: a connection viewers missed, an earlier episode that changes the meaning, lore from outside the show, or a consequence the show never says. It must also pass the Pipeline Limits: told by the narration, never needing one short moment on screen or any sound. Never the main event of the scene, viewers already saw that. Name an official source (fanbook, interview, author note, databook) ONLY if you know its exact content and could name which one it is. If you are not sure, argue from the story itself and never say "the creator confirmed", "Gege said", "in the fanbook" or "officially". A made-up source is worse than no source. The answer must land by the halfway point. No long backstory before it.

ESCALATION (one to two lines): why it's weirder/darker/funnier than it sounds; side detail and the roast line go here.

PUNCHLINE (last line): an ironic twist or one-sentence reframe that hits harder than the answer itself, and calls back to the title so the video comes full circle (style only, do not reuse: title "This is the luckiest Titan in Attack on Titan" ends with "In the end, the only one who found freedom was a Titan nobody cared about."). You may add one very short question after it ("did you catch that?"). NEVER an open loop like "and that's not even the strangest part" (the video ends, so the loop never pays off), NEVER "follow for more", never a flat summary.

SCRIPT STRUCTURE (Format B)
HOOK (the hook field, word for word) → number three → number two → "But number one is..." → PUNCHLINE. One short sentence of setup at most. Every item gets its own receipt and stays about the same character or arc.

SCENE QUERY (for visual search)
scene_query is a pure physical description of the receipt moment — what the screen looks like:

Colors, clothing, hair, actors, props, objects visible.

Kinetic action: car chases, dashing, eyes widening, glowing, debris falling.

No lore jargon; translate effects into visuals.

Describe the BROAD scene or sequence the script references — a whole fight, a dialogue sequence, or setting, never a single frame or one-second beat.

If the receipt is a creator post, behind-the-scenes photo, or comparison image, describe THAT ("a tweet screenshot with…", "side-by-side of two CGI models, left one has…").

YOUTUBE QUERIES (for sourcing raw footage)
Exactly six queries, typed the way a real fan searches — short, natural, two to six words. Not keyword-stuffed. No "creditless / isolated footage / no commentary / raw." Lead with the most recognizable name (actor, character, or film title). One scene per query set. Each query must hit a DIFFERENT result pool.

Query 1 — THE DIRECT MOMENT: "[character/actor] [moment] scene" / "[movie] [character] vs [character] full fight"

Query 2 — EMOTIONAL/VIRAL FRAMING: "[series] [character] [moment] goes hard" / "[actor] acting masterclass [movie]"

Query 3 — HIGH-QUALITY POOL: "[character] [moment] 4k" / "[series] [arc] all fights"

Query 4 — EPISODE/MOVIE POOL: "[series] episode [number] [moment]" / "[movie title] [moment] scene"

Query 5 — DUB vs SUB POOL: (For anime/foreign media, use "[character] [moment] english dub". For English-original live-action/animation, use "[movie/series] [moment] full scene HD")

Query 6 — OFFICIAL/CLIP POOL: "[movie/series] [moment] official clip" / "[movie/series] official trailer"

SCENE ACCURACY RULE: all six chase the exact moment the script references.
TITLE RULE: every query names the series or movie. "[character] edit goes hard" without it pulls unrelated videos (one returned Puss in Boots and drama movies).
SINGLE SOURCE RULE: if the receipt lives in one specific video (a music video, a trailer, one episode), at least three of the six queries name that video by its exact title, because the other pools will not have the scene.

OUTPUT: replace every bracket with real names. No leftover brackets.

FOOTAGE REALITY CHECK (pipeline capability limits)
This pipeline is AUTOMATED: it can only source broad, easy-to-find clips (fights, famous scenes, trailers, edits, compilations). It CANNOT show static screenshots, tweets or photos, and it CANNOT hunt down one exact frame, trim a one-second moment precisely, or isolate audio.

THE GENERIC FOOTAGE TEST: Would this video still work if the editor just played ANY popular clips of the character/scene while the narration carries the fact? If the fact only works when the viewer sees one specific micro-moment at the exact right time — KILL IT.

GREEN — plenty of broad clips exist AND the fact passes the generic footage test. Proceed.

YELLOW: footage of the exact moment is thin, BUT plenty of clips of the character exist and the narration carries the receipt on its own. The pipeline cannot show screenshots, tweets or set photos (it only uses video), so never rely on one. Proceed and flag "receipt is narration only."

RED — fails the generic footage test, OR is audio-dependent, OR has no footage of the character. KILL and take the next candidate.

